# agents/ — Source of Truth

This directory is the canonical home for agents, skills, and rules in **{{PROJECT_NAME}}**. Everything under `.claude/`, `.codex/`, `.opencode/`, `.agents/`, `.github/agents/`, and the root `AGENTS.md` is **generated** from here.

## Editing

1. Edit files here: a role in `roles/`, a skill in `skills/`, a rule in `rules/`.
2. Run `bash agents/scripts/sync_agents.sh` to fan changes out to every enabled surface.
3. Commit `agents/` (and whatever generated output `OUTPUT_TRACKING` in `agentsync.conf` says git tracks).

## Layout

```
agents/
  AGENTS.md          # workspace guide → root AGENTS.md (roles table + shared rules injected)
  agentsync.conf     # surfaces, CLAUDE.md location, tracking policy, Codex options
  roles/             # ONE file per agent → generated for Claude, Codex, OpenCode, Copilot
  rules/             # shared hard rules → .claude/rules/ and the root AGENTS.md
  skills/            # shared skills → .claude/skills/, .agents/skills/, .github/skills/
  claude/            # CLAUDE.md, settings.json, optional Claude-only agents/ and rules/
  codex/configs/     # Codex config fragments → .codex/config.toml
  scripts/           # gen_agents.sh + sync scripts
```

A role file has canonical frontmatter (`name`, `description`, `tier`, `access`, `surfaces`, `skills`) plus optional per-surface blocks (`claude:`, `codex:`, `opencode:`, `github:`) for keys only one tool understands, such as Claude hooks or Copilot handoffs. Roles set no `model`, so each agent inherits the session model. Add `model:` to a surface block only to pin one deliberately.

To give one surface a hand-written agent, drop it in `claude/agents/`, `codex/agents/`, `opencode/agents/`, or `github/agents/`. It's copied verbatim and wins over a generated agent with the same name.

## Never edit generated output directly

It's regenerated on every sync, and your edits will be lost. The sync only touches what it wrote: files you or another tool keep in the same dirs are left alone.

